{"id":127,"date":"2026-07-09T14:28:15","date_gmt":"2026-07-09T14:28:15","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/localhost:8181\/cumberland-valley-builders-skipped-these-two-hvac-details-check-your-mechanicsburg-home\/"},"modified":"2026-07-15T21:01:25","modified_gmt":"2026-07-15T21:01:25","slug":"cumberland-valley-builders-skipped-these-two-hvac-details-check-your-mechanicsburg-home","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/cumberland-valley-builders-skipped-these-two-hvac-details-check-your-mechanicsburg-home\/","title":{"rendered":"Your Mechanicsburg Ranch Got AC as an Afterthought \u2014 It Shows"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text alignfull has-media-on-the-right s-hero-split wp-elements-a1abb5fa53b454ab748e4879f3a30c95\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading s-hero-split__text wp-block-heading\">Your ranch wasn&#039;t built for cooling.<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Post-war suburbs promised turnkey comfort: a furnace in the basement, registers in every room, and a thermostat you set once. But when central air arrived in the 1970s, most Mechanicsburg ranches weren&#039;t replumbed for it.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Book the Assessment<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"979\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/1189776958_8bc7c2113c_b.jpg\" alt=\"victorian house exterior\" class=\"wp-image-196\" style=\"object-fit:cover;height:100%\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/1189776958_8bc7c2113c_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/1189776958_8bc7c2113c_b-300x287.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/1189776958_8bc7c2113c_b-768x734.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;Old House&quot; by Jimmy_Joe, BY, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"s-trust-bar\"><div class=\"s-trust__item\">15+ Years Serving PA<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Licensed &amp; Insured<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Same-Day Emergency<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Local to Your Area<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Problem With Retrofit Air Conditioning<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When a cooling coil is bolted into an existing heating-only duct system, everything changes. Heating systems move more air at lower speeds\u2014around 600 to 800 feet per minute\u2014because warm air rises naturally. Cooling systems need less total airflow but higher velocities\u2014up to 900 feet per minute\u2014to overcome gravity and distribute cold air evenly. The ductwork sized for heating can&#039;t handle cooling loads. Air velocity spikes. Friction increases.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The blower works harder. By the time air reaches the last bedroom, temperature has risen and airflow has dropped to half of what&#039;s needed. The neighborhoods around Simpson Ferry Road and Trindle Road are full of these ranches\u20141,200 to 1,600 square feet, built between 1955 and 1975. The HVAC systems in most are original or first-generation replacements, undersized by modern standards and installed before Manual J load calculations were standard practice.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bedrooms at the end of duct runs stay warm. The living room overcools. Upstairs bonus rooms get whatever air is left over after the rest of the house takes its share.<\/p>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text\" style=\"grid-template-columns:50% auto\"><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"800\" height=\"600\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation.jpg\" alt=\"mini split heat pump\" class=\"wp-image-202\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation.jpg 800w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;Ductless Mini Split Heat Pump Installation&quot; by Vernon Air Con, BY-SA, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Undersized Ductwork Actually Means<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In Mechanicsburg ranches, the main trunk line often runs down the center of the basement ceiling, with short branch ducts feeding rooms on either side. The trunk might be six or eight inches in diameter, adequate for a 60,000-BTU furnace but marginal for a three-ton air conditioner. The branch ducts, often flex duct kinked around joists and plumbing, add resistance.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Fixing undersized ductwork means either increasing duct size or reducing system capacity. Increasing size is expensive\u2014it requires tearing out drywall, reframing ceiling cavities, and replacing trunk line segments. Most homeowners balk at the disruption and cost. Reducing capacity sounds counterintuitive, but a properly sized smaller system that runs longer cycles often outperforms an oversized system that short-cycles.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The longer run times allow air to circulate fully, temperatures to equalize, and humidity to drop.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Return Air Problem Nobody Addresses<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-indent has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most Mechanicsburg ranches have a single return grille in the hallway, feeding directly into the furnace. When the furnace runs, it pushes air into every room, but the return only pulls air from the hallway. Rooms with closed doors develop positive pressure, which forces conditioned air out through window gaps and attic hatches.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The hallway develops negative pressure, which pulls unconditioned air in through the return grille and sends it straight back without ever conditioning the rooms that need it. Adding return grilles in bedrooms or transfer grilles above doors balances airflow and prevents pressure imbalances. This straightforward modification transforms system performance\u2014rooms that were always too hot or too cold suddenly stabilize, and the furnace stops cycling every ten minutes.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text has-media-on-the-right\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why Your Attic Matters More Than Your Furnace<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ranch homes have large attic surfaces relative to their conditioned volume\u2014more roof area per square foot of living space than two-story homes. If the attic has the original six inches of fiberglass batts, common in 1960s construction, the insulation value is around R-19. Modern code calls for R-49 in Pennsylvania. An under-insulated ranch can lose thirty percent of its heating load through the ceiling.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">No furnace upgrade will fix that gap. Air sealing matters as much as insulation depth. Penetrations for recessed lights, plumbing vents, and electrical boxes create pathways for conditioned air to escape into the attic. Even a quarter-inch gap around a ceiling fixture can leak hundreds of cubic feet of air per hour when the furnace runs. Sealing these penetrations with expanding foam before adding insulation stops the leakage.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The insulation then slows conductive heat transfer rather than sitting on top of moving air. Proper attic ventilation also matters\u2014ridge vents, soffit vents, and gable vents keep attic temperatures moderate in summer, reducing the cooling load and extending roof shingle life.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/6183733_163f1c5150_b.jpg\" alt=\"victorian house exterior\" class=\"wp-image-195\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/6183733_163f1c5150_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/6183733_163f1c5150_b-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/6183733_163f1c5150_b-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;This Old House&quot; by Jan Tik, BY, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--wide\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">HVAC Solutions for Mid-Century Ranches<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-cards-3 is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ductwork Retrofit<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Increase trunk line size, add return grilles, seal leaks, and rebalance dampers to improve airflow without replacing equipment.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Zone Dampers<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Motorized dampers and multiple thermostats deliver independent temperature control to different areas of the home.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Mini-Split Addition<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Single-zone ductless units condition bonus rooms and additions without stressing the main system.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Equipment Upsizing<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Replace furnace and air conditioner with larger-capacity units sized for expanded square footage and modern loads.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Envelope Upgrades<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Attic insulation, air sealing, and crawl space weatherization reduce heating and cooling loads before touching HVAC.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Heat Pump Conversion<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Modern heat pumps with SEER ratings above 16 provide heating and cooling efficiency gains in Cumberland County climate.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Assessment Process<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-steps\">\n<li>Load Calculation \u2014 Manual J analysis determines actual heating and cooling requirements based on square footage, insulation, air leakage, and solar gain.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Ductwork Audit \u2014 We measure trunk and branch duct sizes, identify leaks and resistance points, and model airflow to pinpoint comfort problems.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Envelope Inspection \u2014 Attic insulation depth, air sealing condition, and ventilation paths are evaluated to separate building envelope from mechanical system issues.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Options Report \u2014 You receive specific repair or replacement recommendations with costs, efficiency gains, and comfort improvements for each option.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-stats is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">30%<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Typical heating loss through under-insulated attic<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">R-19 \u2192 R-49<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Insulation upgrade needed for code compliance<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">$3\u20135K<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Cost of two-zone system vs. full replacement<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">15+<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">SEER rating for modern heat pump efficiency<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What We Actually Look At<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Every Mechanicsburg ranch tells the same story through its mechanical details. We know what to look for because we&#039;ve worked on hundreds of homes built to the same post-war template.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-icon-list\">\n<li>Furnace age, efficiency rating, and blower type\u2014single-speed blowers can&#039;t support added cooling<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Ductwork diameter, material, and routing\u2014galvanized runs from the 1960s often leak at every joint<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Return air pathways\u2014single hallway grilles create pressure imbalances in multi-zone homes<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Attic insulation depth and air sealing\u2014R-19 batts and unsealed light fixtures waste thirty percent of heating load<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Previous modifications\u2014added bedrooms, finished attics, or HVAC extensions that strain the original system<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Thermostat location and type\u2014central hallway placement misses conditions in corner bedrooms<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Common Questions from Mechanicsburg Homeowners<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--bordered has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Can I add air conditioning without replacing my furnace?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Yes, as long as the furnace blower can handle the airflow requirements of the cooling coil. Many older furnaces have single-speed blowers that won&#039;t move enough air for efficient cooling. If the furnace is newer with a variable-speed blower, adding a coil and condenser is straightforward. A contractor will evaluate airflow, duct capacity, and electrical service to confirm compatibility.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Why does my house feel humid even when the air conditioner runs?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Short cycling is the most common cause. If the system cools the air quickly but shuts off before running long enough to remove moisture, indoor humidity stays high. Oversized equipment, dirty coils, and low refrigerant charge all contribute to short cycling. Fixing the problem usually involves addressing the root cause\u2014resizing equipment, cleaning coils, or adjusting airflow\u2014rather than just turning the thermostat down.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Is it worth upgrading to a high-efficiency furnace in a ranch house?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">It depends on the home&#039;s envelope and your heating costs. If the home is well-insulated and sealed, a high-efficiency furnace delivers measurable savings. If the envelope leaks and the attic is under-insulated, fixing those problems first provides better ROI. In many cases, a mid-efficiency furnace paired with envelope upgrades outperforms a high-efficiency furnace in a leaky house.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>What causes the temperature difference between my upstairs and downstairs?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In split-level ranches, the stack effect causes warm air to rise through the home. If ductwork doesn&#039;t balance return air between levels, the upper floors overheat in summer and the lower levels get overcooled. Adding a second return duct on the opposite end of the basement or installing zone dampers solves this without replacing equipment.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-nearby has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Also serving nearby:<\/strong> <a href=\"\/one-thermostat-cant-run-a-three-story-carlisle-foursquare\/\">Carlisle<\/a> \u00b7 <a href=\"\/living-near-the-susquehanna-means-humidity-here-is-how-harrisburg-homes-beat-it\/\">Harrisburg<\/a><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Our Work in Your Neighborhood<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-gallery alignwide has-nested-images columns-3 is-cropped s-gallery wp-block-gallery-1 is-layout-flex wp-block-gallery-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" data-id=\"188\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b.jpg\" alt=\"hvac technician repair\" class=\"wp-image-188\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;AC Service&quot; by SuperTech HVAC Services, BY-SA, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" data-id=\"194\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/6065540454_7be71d4c